### 1. Production Scenario
An international gaming platform runs an Aurora PostgreSQL 16 cluster storing 450M user session rows. A nightly reconciliation job executes an un-indexed batch update modifying 50M records across 15 minutes (`UPDATE user_sessions SET status = 'INACTIVE' WHERE last_active < NOW() - INTERVAL '90 days'`). The active Debezium connector logical replication slot (`debezium_cdc_slot`) pins the Write-Ahead Log (WAL) segment retention. Because Debezium's single-threaded event parsing loop cannot consume the 85 GB of raw WAL generated per minute over the network, `pg_wal` disk usage explodes from 40 GB to 1.8 TB in 35 minutes, crossing the 95% storage threshold. Aurora enters storage auto-expansion throttling, write latency on live gameplay sessions surges from 3ms to 12,000ms, and Postgres initiates emergency read-only failover, taking down the multi-player matchmaking service globally.

