### 3. Deep Systems Analysis
Postgres logical replication relies on `pgoutput` decoding the WAL sequentially. When a monolithic transaction updates 50M rows, the entire transaction is written into WAL before commit. The replication slot cannot advance its confirmed restart LSN (`confirmed_flush_lsn`) until the transaction commits and is decoded. If WAL generation exceeds network transmission bandwidth ($R_{	ext{WAL}} > B_{	ext{network}}$), storage growth is unbounded: $\Delta S = \int (R_{	ext{WAL}} - B_{	ext{network}}) dt$. Setting `max_slot_wal_keep_size = 256GB` enforces an absolute circuit breaker: if Debezium lags beyond 256GB, Postgres drops or invalidates the slot rather than crashing the primary database.

